About us
The Financial Times is one of the world’s leading news organisations, globally recognised for its authority, integrity and accuracy, with a mission to deliver quality information and services worldwide.
At the FT, curiosity thrives and ambitious thinking is rewarded. Here, you’re given the chance to reach millions, create work that matters and deliver impartial journalism in a polarised world.
In our warm, collaborative culture, you’ll connect with a diverse community of experts who support your growth, career aspirations and wellbeing.
Your future at the FT will be filled with opportunities that challenge and inspire you. With no fixed path, you’ll discover new skills and forge a career that can take you anywhere.
Build a newsworthy career at the FT.
&nbsp;
Our commitment to inclusion and fairness
We believe everyone should have a fair opportunity to contribute, succeed, and reach their full potential. That means ensuring everyone is heard, respected, and valued, while identifying and removing barriers that may get in the way. Our commitment is to be an employer role model, grounded in inclusion and fairness for all.
We want every application to receive fair and thoughtful consideration; therefore, all applications are reviewed by a member of our recruitment team, not by AI.
&nbsp;
The role
The Financial Times is looking for a smart and energetic business editor to join the Companies desk in London. This is an important role on the desk, helping steer the coverage of key sectors, ensure the smooth running of the desk and editing some of our highest profile stories, whether splash-worthy scoops or corporate deep dives.&nbsp;
This person will be liaising with correspondents around the world, helping steer coverage of everything from banks and oil majors, to carmakers, luxury giants and defence start-ups. This is a wide-ranging job, where a good command of corporate reporting, smart news judgement and the ability to make complicated stories sing are essential.&nbsp;
Business knowledge and a reporting background are important qualifications. This person needs to be able to confidently handle longer investigative stories and take care of legally contentious pieces.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Key responsibilities
• Commission sharp news stories and original analysis in conjunction with other editors
• Help maintain a healthy pipeline of scoops and reads that will populate prime slots on the homepage
• Edit copy so stories have appropriate length, pace and punch
• Closely co-ordinate with other desks, especially New York, Hong Kong and Markets
• Project stories to the main news desk with smart headlines and strong graphics to maximise their audience
• Work on longer term data projects with the long form and visual journalism teams
• Weekend and some early morning shifts are required as part of the team rota
&nbsp;
What we are looking for
The right candidate will be news driven, a team player and will ideally have been an accomplished reporter and writer of corporate stories. Good communication and planning skills are essential. This editor must be comfortable exercising editorial judgement on legally contentious stories and be experienced working to tight deadlines and producing stories very quickly on breaking news. Experience editing long form would also be a plus.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Please submit your application by the end of the day, Sunday 22nd November 2026.
&nbsp;
The use of AI
Original reporting, great writing and intelligent commentary lie at the heart of FT journalism. Our readers trust us to produce quality journalism that is accurate, fair and insightful. This is why FT editorial staff do not by default use artificial intelligence (AI) tools to write articles or create other reader-facing content. For specific and pre-approved cases, AI can be harnessed for certain tasks, such as assisting data research for investigations.&nbsp;&nbsp;
We expect applicants to the FT newsroom to refrain from using AI in their test submissions, and any written materials should be a genuine reflection of their own knowledge, skills and experiences. We encourage candidates only to employ AI as a tool to help with research and interview readiness for a role.&nbsp;
Candidates should be aware that the use of AI throughout the application process is monitored to ensure a fair and transparent hiring process for all.
&nbsp;
What’s in it for you? Our benefits
Our benefits vary by location but we are committed to providing best-in-class perks across all our offices. These include generous annual leave, medical cover, inclusive parental leave packages, subsidised gym memberships and opportunities to give back to the community. Full details of our benefits are available here.
FT Newsroom staff are expected to work onsite a minimum of three days per week, subject to role requirements and regular review. While flexible working requests will be considered, not all patterns are suitable for Editorial roles.
&nbsp;
Accessibility
We are a disability confident employer and Valuable 500 signatory.
Please let us know if you require any reasonable adjustments/personalisation as part of the application process or to enable you to attend an interview. If you would like to discuss your requirements or have any questions, email talent@ft.com and a member of our team will be happy to help.
*LI-RS1

About us
GoCardless, a Mollie company, is a global leader in bank payments. Over 100,000 businesses, from start-ups to household names, use GoCardless to collect, manage and send bank payments through Direct Debit, real-time payments and open banking. With US$130bn+ processed annually across 30+ countries, we handle recurring and one-off payments without the chasing, stress, or expensive fees. Our end-to-end payment platform also features AI-powered solutions to improve payment success and reduce fraud, alongside connections to over 350 platforms businesses use everyday.
We are headquartered in the UK, with teams and operations spanning North America, Europe and Asia-Pacific.&nbsp; For more information, please visit www.gocardless.com and follow us on LinkedIn @GoCardless.
Mollie is the leading payments and financial services partner for business, rooted in Europe, with global reach.
The role
We are looking for a Treasury Assistant to join the financial reporting team. Working closely with the head of financial reporting and the treasury manager, you will have the opportunity to get involved in the treasury management of GoCardless.&nbsp;
What excites you&nbsp;
• Preparing weekly and daily cash flows and performing liquidity controls to ensure sufficient liquidity within the groups entities.&nbsp;
• Collaborating with other teams including AP and management reporting ensuring accurate treasury balance forecasting.&nbsp;
• Ownership of monitoring the groups bank accounts and reconciling accounts to balances.&nbsp;
• Provide cover for bank reconciliations.&nbsp;
• Supporting the treasury manager and head of financial reporting as required.&nbsp;
• Support ad hoc projects as and when required.
What excites us&nbsp;&nbsp;
• 2-3 years of treasury experience&nbsp;
• Intermediate excel / google sheet skills
• Ability to use AI and to automate tasks and processes&nbsp;
• Experience in treasury forecasting
• Ability to prioritise time and stay organised in a fast-paced environment&nbsp;
• Experience with multi-currency treasury operations
• Ability to produce accurate work and consistently deliver to reporting deadlines&nbsp;
• Team player but ability to work independently&nbsp;
We don’t expect you to meet every requirement. If you’re excited by this role, we encourage you to apply.&nbsp;
Base salary range: £32,000 - £48,000
Base salary ranges are based on role, job level, location, and market data.&nbsp; Please note that whilst we strive to offer competitive compensation, our approach is to pay between the minimum and the mid-point (£32,000 - £40,000) of the pay range until performance can be assessed in role. Offers will take into account level of experience, interview assessment, budgets and parity between you and fellow employees at GoCardless doing similar work.
(some of) The good stuff
• Wellbeing - stay healthy with dedicated support and medical cover
• Lunch- based on the location, you either get a meal voucher or a paid lunch when working from the office
• Work away scheme - gives you the option to work away from your country of residence for up to 20 working days in any 12-month period
